{"name":"God of Rain","hex":"#4466cc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"god","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Godié."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deity or supreme being; a supernatural, typically immortal, being with superior powers, to which personhood is attributed.","An idol.\nA representation of a deity, especially a statue or statuette.\n Something or someone particularly revered, worshipped, idealized, admired and/or followed."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/god"},{"term":"rain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Condensed water falling from a cloud.","Any matter moving or falling, usually through air, and especially if liquid or otherwise figuratively identifiable with raindrops."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To have rain fall from the sky.","To fall as or like rain."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rain"}],"entries":[{"title":"List of rain deities","blocks":[{"text":"There are many different gods of rain in different religions:"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/05MillanSchoolWall.JPG/330px-05MillanSchoolWall.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_rain_deities","wikidata":"Q3027571"},{"title":"God","blocks":[{"text":"A supreme deity, supreme being, supreme god, or simply just God, is a deity within theistic belief systems which is believed to be the ultimate cause of all things in existence. As such, it is seen as the creator, sustainer, and ruler of the universe. God is often thought of as incorporeal and independent of the material creation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Michelangelo%2C_Creation_of_Adam_04.jpg/330px-Michelangelo%2C_Creation_of_Adam_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/God","wikidata":"Q190"},{"title":"Rain","blocks":[{"text":"Rain or rainfall is a form of atmospheric precipitation composed of droplets of water aerosols that have condensed from atmospheric water vapor and fall by gravity. Depending on the intensity, rain can be described as a drizzle, a shower, a cloudburst or a downpour. It is also often accompanied by other weather phenomena such as strong wind (rainstorm) and lightning (thunderstorm), and may be mixed with hail/ice pellet, snow (sleet) and sandstorm. Rain is a major component of the water cycle and is responsible for depositing most of the fresh water on Earth. It provides water for hydroelectric power plants, crop irrigation, and suitable conditions for many types of ecosystems."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Hard_rain_on_a_roof.jpg/330px-Hard_rain_on_a_roof.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rain","wikidata":"Q7925"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Istanbul%2C_Hagia_Sophia%2C_Allah.jpg/500px-Istanbul%2C_Hagia_Sophia%2C_Allah.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Istanbul%2C_Hagia_Sophia%2C_Allah.jpg/500px-Istanbul%2C_Hagia_Sophia%2C_Allah.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIstanbul%2C_Hagia_Sophia%2C_Allah.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Tetragrammaton_Sefardi.jpg/500px-Tetragrammaton_Sefardi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Tetragrammaton_Sefardi.jpg/500px-Tetragrammaton_Sefardi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATetragrammaton_Sefardi.jpg"}